# keys.zd — document-level keystrokes (spec §16.3.7a).
#
# `on keydown` bound to an `Input` hears what that field was sent. Ten of
# the portfolio's `useEffect`s want the other thing: `Escape` closes the
# dialog wherever the reader's focus happens to be, and the arrow keys
# move the board. There is no element to attach that to, because the
# element it listens to is the page.
#
# That is a strictly larger capability, and it is the reason this file
# exists. Four things it demonstrates.
#
#   1. **It names its key, and it receives nothing.** There is no `with`
#      binder and the production has none to write, so a handler cannot
#      ask what was typed, count keystrokes it did not name, or time
#      them. It learns one bit about one key written in the source. `on
#      keydown with stroke` still binds a payload, unchanged, because a
#      field's own keystrokes were addressed to that field.
#
#   2. **The emitted listener stands down while a field has focus.**
#      That is what makes a printable key safe: `on key "r"` below cannot
#      see the `r` in a password, because while any input, textarea,
#      select or contenteditable has focus it returns without looking.
#      Type `r` into the field and `rescues` does not move; click away
#      and it does. Nothing here asks for that — the compiler emits it.
#
#   3. **Its position is its lifetime, and only that.** It is refused
#      indented under an element, because `on key` under a `Button` reads
#      as "while this button has focus" and does not mean it. It goes
#      beside the elements — at the top of a view, a component body or a
#      branch. The `Escape` handler below sits inside `if open`, so it
#      exists only while the dialog does, and closing the dialog removes
#      the listener from the document. One that outlived its view would
#      keep firing into a graph nothing renders: a leak and a wrong
#      answer at once.
#
#   4. **A component owns its own.** `Board` declares `on key "g"` and
#      two instances would be two listeners, disposed with their
#      instances — the same rule component state already has.
#
# What is deliberately absent, one line each: modifier chords
# (`control k`); `keyup`; and `resize`, `scroll` and `pointermove`, which
# are not events at all but quantities, and want the shape `every` gave a
# clock rather than the shape a keystroke has. Also absent:
# `preventDefault`, so the arrow keys below still scroll the page. That is
# §16.3.7's standing decision and not this file's to change.

component Board with label
    state glow is client Whole starting 0

    Row
        Text label
        Text glow
    on key "g"
        add 1 to glow

state open    is client Truth starting no
state cursor  is client Whole starting 0
state rescues is client Whole starting 0
state note    is client Text  starting ""
state lastKey is client Text  starting "none"

view title is "Keys"
    Column
        Heading "Document keys"

        Row
            Text "cursor: "
            Text cursor
        Row
            Text "rescues: "
            Text rescues
        Row
            Text "last key in the field: "
            Text lastKey

        # A handler on an element is unchanged: this one hears only what
        # this field was sent, and it binds the payload.
        Row
            Text "type here: "
            Input note
                on keydown with stroke
                    set lastKey to stroke.key

        Board "board:"

        Button "open the dialog"
            on click
                set open to yes

        if open
            Column
                Text "press Escape to close"
                Button "close"
                    on click
                        set open to no
            # Inside the branch, so it is registered when the dialog
            # appears and removed when it goes.
            on key "Escape"
                set open to no

    # Beside `Column`, not under it: these live as long as the page.
    # `ArrowLeft` and `ArrowRight` are named keys; `r` is a single
    # character, which is the other admissible shape.
    on key "ArrowRight"
        add 1 to cursor
    on key "ArrowLeft"
        subtract 1 from cursor
    on key "r"
        add 1 to rescues
